The prototype runs the full design on synthetic data built with the same problems. It covers {{n_deals}} deals, {{n_loans}} loans and {{n_loan_months}} loan-months from 2021-10 to 2026-09, in 2 different trustee formats. Rates follow the RBA path and house prices follow suburb indices. 90+ arrears rise from {{arrears_90p_first_pct}} to a peak of {{arrears_90p_peak_pct}} in {{arrears_90p_peak_month}}, then fall back to {{arrears_90p_latest_pct}}.

**The cleaning is tested row by row.** The raw trustee files hold {{n_raw_rows}} rows, including {{n_resubmission_duplicates}} resubmitted duplicates, and one format reports its {{n_called_deals_b}} clean-up calls as ordinary repayments. After cleaning, the canonical table matches the simulated truth on all {{n_loan_months}} loan-months, with {{canonical_truth_mismatches}} differences.

**The compiler is tested against hand-written SQL.** I wrote {{gold_total}} queries by hand and compared them with what the compiler produced. All {{gold_matched}} gave the same answers, with the largest gap ({{gold_worst_diff}}) far too small to matter. Every compiled query also works in a form Oracle can run.

**Volume isn't a problem.** With the data copied 36 times, to {{rows_36x}} loan-months, each of the 34 test questions with a numeric answer comes back in under {{x36_max_default_s}} seconds on a single computer. Even with the computer limited to 1 processor core, the slowest takes {{x36_max_single_s}} seconds. These timings come from DuckDB, the database used in the prototype. Speed on Oracle depends on how the tables are set up, which I didn't test.

